<article class="post">
<header class="post-header">
<h1>Why internal links matter</h1>
<div class="post-meta">
<address>
<ul class="people" role="list" aria-label="Written by">
<li class="person"><span><a rel="author" href="/blog/authors/asif/">Asif</a><span class="person-role">Founder of SerpDad</span></span></li>
</ul>
</address>
<p>Published <time datetime="2026-09-14">14 September 2026</time>. Updated <time datetime="2026-10-20">20 October 2026</time></p>
<p>Filed under <a href="/blog/category/on-page-seo/">On-page SEO</a></p>
</div>
</header>
<div class="post-body">
<p>An internal link is a link from one page on your site to another page on the same site. It sounds too simple to matter. It matters a lot.</p>
<h2 id="readers-use-them-to-keep-going">Readers use them to keep going <a class="heading-link" href="#readers-use-them-to-keep-going" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>Someone who finishes a post is interested. If the post ends without pointing anywhere, they leave. One link to a related post gives them a next step, and gives you a second page view.</p>
<h2 id="search-engines-use-them-to-find-and-rank-pages">Search engines use them to find and rank pages <a class="heading-link" href="#search-engines-use-them-to-find-and-rank-pages" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>A crawler finds new pages by following links. A post that nothing links to is hard to find and looks unimportant. A post that ten other posts link to looks like something your site cares about. That is how you build <a href="/blog/glossary/topical-authority/">Topical authority</a>.</p>
<h2 id="how-to-add-them-well">How to add them well <a class="heading-link" href="#how-to-add-them-well" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<ul>
<li>Link from new posts to older ones, and go back to add links from older posts to new ones.</li>
<li>Use anchor text that says what the reader gets: &quot;how to write a title tag&quot;, not &quot;click here&quot;.</li>
<li>Use the same anchor text for the same page. If one phrase points to two different pages, search engines cannot tell which one you mean.</li>
</ul>
<p>Start with one link per post. For example, once your links are in place, read <a href="/blog/how-to-write-a-title-tag/">how to write a title tag</a> next.</p>

</div>
</article>
